.\" allowed-projects(5) -- an operator's project allowlist for Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst
.\" and the RPM %prep), like ai-tools(1). The grammar described here is the one
.\" conf.lib.sh implements for the path-list files, shared by every reader.
.TH ALLOWED-PROJECTS 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
allowed-projects \- the project directories an operator's agent sessions may start in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each operator keeps one
.B allowed-projects
file, and it decides two things for that operator:
where a session launched by that account may start, and which agent-written paths
the ownership hand-back returns to that account.
A session starts only inside a directory the file lists, and never inside a subtree
a
.RB \(oq ! \(cq
line excludes.
.PP
The file is a launch gate and an ownership registry, not a read boundary.
Once a session is running, ordinary file permissions and the SELinux policy decide
what it reads and writes.
.PP
The file is owned by the operator at mode
.BR 0600 ,
inside a
.B 0700
directory, so no other account reads it.
.B "ai-tools-admin operators add"
creates it with a header and no entry, so no session can start anywhere until an
entry is added.
The
.BR ai-tools (1)
commands add, park and remove entries in place; edit by hand to review, to
comment, or to carve a subtree out of a project.
.SH GRAMMAR
One entry per line.
.TP
.I /home/op/project
An allow entry: a literal directory.
Sessions may start in it and in every directory under it.
.TP
.I /home/op/project   # why
An end-of-line comment.
A
.B #
at the start of a line, or after whitespace, begins a comment.
.TP
.B "\(dq/home/op/ai works\(dq"
One layer of matched quotes carries a path containing a space or a literal
.BR # .
.TP
.B !/home/op/project/vendor
An exclusion.
The
.B !
precedes the quotes of a quoted path:
.BR "!\(dq/a b\(dq" .
.PP
Blank lines are ignored.
Every entry is resolved to its real path before it is compared, so an entry
reached through a symbolic link or a trailing slash matches the directory it
names.
.SS Allow entries
An allow entry is a literal directory and covers its contents.
A glob in an allow line does not match any path, so it does not start a session;
.B "ai-tools --list"
reports such a line as unusable.
An entry naming a directory that no longer exists is skipped, and reported as stale.
.SS Exclusions
An exclusion outranks an allow entry for the same path and for every path under it.
A plain exclusion covers its contents; one carrying
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.B [
or
.B ]
is matched as a glob against the whole path, as written.
Only an exclusion may use a glob.
.PP
An exclusion means one of two things, and the position decides which:
.TP
.B a carve-out
An exclusion inside a listed project withholds that subtree from the agent.
The launch refuses the subtree as a working directory, and no hand-back runs
there.
Lift it by editing the line;
.B "ai-tools --project-enable"
refuses it, since removing a carve-out widens what the agent reaches.
.TP
.B a parked project
An exclusion with no listed project enclosing it takes a claimed project out of
service.
The entry, the permissions and the label stay in place, so
.B "ai-tools --project-enable"
restores it.
.SH ENTRY STATES
A path is in one of three states, and the
.BR ai-tools (1)
commands name all three:
.TP
.B listed
An allow line names the path.
Sessions may start there.
.TP
.B disabled
A
.B !
line names the path.
No session starts there.
While a project is parked, the hand-back does not restore files written under it,
so park a project only once its sessions have ended.
.TP
.B absent
Neither line names the path.
It is not a project.
.PP
A listed path can still be unreachable, when an ancestor is parked or a glob
exclusion matches it.
A command that changes an entry reports the entry's new state and names the
other line where one still blocks the path.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
This file, one per operator.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
The operator's secret-name patterns, kept beside it with the same ownership.
.TP
.I /var/opt/ai-tools/sandbox-projects/
The sandbox-clone area; a clone made by
.B "ai-tools --sandbox-create"
is listed here like any other project.
.SH EXAMPLES
A project, a carve-out inside it, and a parked project:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/home/op/webapp
!/home/op/webapp/vendor        # third-party code the agent does not touch
!/home/op/oldapp               # parked: ai-tools --project-enable restores it
.EE
.RE
.PP
A path containing a space, a quoted exclusion inside it, and a glob exclusion
of every dependency directory one level under another project:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
"/home/op/ai works"
!"/home/op/ai works/build"
!/home/op/webapp/*/node_modules
.EE
.RE
.PP
Register a project, review the file, and park a project without editing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai\-tools \-\-project\-claim ~/src/webapp
ai\-tools \-\-list
ai\-tools \-\-project\-disable ~/src/oldapp
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools (1),
.BR ai\-tools\-admin (8),
.BR operator.conf (5),
.BR secret\-patterns (5)
.PP
The lifecycle guide covers claiming a tree in place against working in a
sandbox clone, and what each prompt grants:
.IR /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/project\-lifecycle.md .
